       "content": "# Popular Web Game Engines\n\n## 2D-Focused\n\n### **Phaser**\n- **Pros:** Massive community, huge plugin ecosystem, excellent docs, works in any browser, huge asset library, very active development, free/open-source.\n- **Cons:** Can feel bloated for simple projects, performance ceiling for very complex scenes, the API has shifted between major versions (2\u21923), not ideal for 3D.\n- **Outdated risk:** Low. It",
       "name": null,
       "reasoning": "The user is asking about popular game engines for web-based game development. Let me think about what's currently popular and relevant for web game development.\n\nKey web game engines to consider:\n\n1. **Phaser** - Very popular 2D HTML5 game framework\n2. **PixiJS** - 2D WebGL rendering engine (not a full game engine but often used as one)\n3. **Three.js** - 3D WebGL library (can be used for games)\n4. **Babylon.js** - 3D game engine by Microsoft\n5. **PlayCanvas** - 3D web game engine\n6. **Godot** - Can export to web (WASM)\n7. **Unity** - Can export to web (WASM) but with caveats\n8. **Cocos Creator** - 2D/3D web game engine, very popular in Asia\n9. **Construct** - No-code/low-code web game engine\n10. **Kaboom.js** - Newer, simpler 2D game engine\n11. **Excalibur.js** - 2D game engine\n12. **Defold** - Can target web\n\nLet me focus on the most popular and relevant ones for WEB specifically:\n\n- Phaser (2D, very popular)\n- PixiJS (2D rendering, very popular)\n- Three.js (3D, extremely popular)\n- Babylon.js (3D, Microsoft-backed)\n- PlayCanvas (3D, cloud-based)\n- Godot (2D/3D, exports to web via WASM)\n- Unity (2D/3D, exports to web via WASM)\n- Cocos Creator (2D/3D, very popular in Asia)\n- Construct 3 (no-code, web)\n- Kaboom.js (2D, newer)\n\nLet me think about \"unlikely to be outdated soon\" -",
